# Coding Standards

## Scope and simplicity

- Make the smallest change that solves the problem. Do not refactor or expand scope unless asked.
- Prefer clear, direct code over abstractions used only once.
- Match existing naming, structure, and patterns in the codebase before introducing new ones.

## Code quality

- Keep functions and modules focused on one responsibility.
- Use descriptive names; avoid abbreviations unless they are project-standard.
- Add comments only for non-obvious business logic or tricky technical constraints—not for obvious code.

## Testing

- Add or update tests when changing behavior that can break silently.
- Skip tests that only restate implementation details (e.g. asserting a getter returns what was set).
- Prefer testing user-visible behavior and edge cases over internal wiring.

## Git and commits

- Do not create commits unless explicitly requested.
- Write commit messages in complete sentences: what changed and why (1–2 sentences).
- Never commit secrets, credentials, or local env files (`.env`, keys, tokens).

## Dependencies and files

- Do not add new dependencies without a clear need; prefer the standard library or what the project already uses.
- Do not create markdown or doc files unless the user asks for them.
